Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS)
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Irregular menses, Hyperandrogenism signs, Polycystic ovaries on ultrasound

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-034
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe emotional distress and acute pelvic cramping following a prolonged period of amenorrhoea.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute, severe psychological distress (panic attack) combined with sudden, sharp pelvic cramping after 4 months of absent periods, causing extreme anxiety regarding an ectopic pregnancy or miscarriage. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out a pregnancy-related complication, provide acute analgesia, and de-escalate the severe psychiatric distress before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I haven't had a period in four months, and suddenly I have these sharp cramps and I am terrified I am having a miscarriage alone in my apartment.
History of Present Illness: The patient, aged 26, reports a long history of oligoovulation manifesting as irregular, unpredictable menstrual cycles, most recently having no menses for 4 months. Tonight, she experienced the sudden onset of sharp, severe pelvic cramping, triggering a severe panic attack due to fears of an ectopic pregnancy. She exhibits clinical signs of hyperandrogenism, including treatment-resistant severe cystic acne along the jawline and mild facial hirsutism. She also notes recent central obesity with increased waist circumference. She reports a history of subfertility primarily due to chronic anovulation.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, but highly agitated, crying, and pacing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; acute psychological crisis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/85 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient appears flushed, tearful, hyperventilating, and is clutching a heating pad to her abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals diffuse, mild bilateral lower quadrant tenderness to deep palpation; no rebound, rigidity, or focal peritoneal signs. Examination of the face and neck reveals severe cystic acne and acanthosis nigricans (dark, velvety hyperpigmentation) at the posterior neck, indicating underlying severe insulin resistance. Point-of-care urine pregnancy test is unequivocally negative.
Environmental Observations: Neatly kept apartment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) - Status: Acute anovulatory cramping / Dysmenorrhoea flare with acute panic attack
Differentials:

Ectopic Pregnancy / Miscarriage - Definitively ruled out by the negative highly sensitive point-of-care urine beta-hCG test.

Ovarian Torsion - Unlikely given the diffuse, bilateral, and relatively mild objective tenderness on palpation, lacking the classic severe, unilateral, rigid peritonism of torsion.

Appendicitis - Ruled out by the bilateral nature of the pain and absence of focal right lower quadrant tenderness or fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute psychological crisis triggered by sudden pelvic cramping on a background of classic, undiagnosed Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (irregular menses, clinical hyperandrogenism, insulin resistance signs). The acute fear of a pregnancy complication drove severe hyperventilation and distress. Objective clinical assessment definitively ruled out ectopic pregnancy or miscarriage via a negative urine test, instantly resolving the immediate life-threat concern. Urgent after-hours intervention was required to safely exclude these emergencies, provide robust clinical reassurance to break the panic cycle, and administer simple analgesia, preventing an unnecessary, highly distressed emergency department presentation for a benign anovulatory flare.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted immediate point-of-care pregnancy testing, providing the negative result directly to the patient to halt the panic attack. Administered oral naproxen 500mg for the pelvic cramping. Generated a routine referral for a comprehensive transvaginal ultrasound (to assess for subcapsular follicles) and fasting blood work (HbA1c, testosterone, lipids) to formally diagnose PCOS.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the acute panic, practice grounding by identifying five things you can see and four things you can touch; actively engaging your senses will immediately interrupt the anxiety loop. To address the underlying hormonal imbalance, commit to a daily 30-minute brisk walk; recognizing that even mild weight loss significantly improves insulin resistance will motivate consistent daily exercise.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 2 weeks to discuss ultrasound/blood results and initiate targeted PCOS management (e.g., metformin, combined oral contraceptives).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if the abdominal pain suddenly becomes sharp, severe, and localized to one specific side, or if you develop a fever or begin vomiting continuously.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The pregnancy test is absolutely negative tonight; the pain is a heavy menstrual cramp trying to start after a long delay.
Clinical Course: The cramping should ease significantly with the naproxen. You may experience a heavy, irregular bleed over the next few days as the thickened lining sheds.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the pain becomes sudden, sharp, and localized to one side, or if you develop a high fever.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You are not pregnant and not having a miscarriage. Your symptoms—the missed periods, the acne, the dark skin on your neck, and the cramping tonight—are classic signs of Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS). Because you haven't had a period in four months, the lining of your womb is thick and trying to shed, causing these severe cramps. The medication will ease the pain, but we need to formally diagnose and manage the PCOS to regulate your hormones.
Actionable Steps: Take the naproxen with food to manage the cramps tonight. Use your heating pad. Schedule the ultrasound and blood tests this week, and see your regular GP to discuss long-term management.

